  "notes": "Open folders in Tributary from File Explorer, choose which tags to push, and stage a new folder's files one by one.\n\n## New\n\n- **Open in Tributary, from File Explorer:** on Windows, right-click a folder, or the empty space in an open one, and choose **Open in Tributary**. On Windows 11 it's under **Show more options**. The folder opens in a tab of its own, in the Tributary that's already running if there is one. A folder that isn't a repository yet offers to make it one.\n\n- **Push tags:** pushing a branch doesn't push its tags. **Push tags…**, in the menu beside Push, asks the remote which tags it has and lists the ones it's missing. Those on commits it already has, usually releases, are ticked. One on a commit only you have isn't, as a pushed tag is hard to take back.\n\n## Better\n\n- **The files in a new folder:** a folder you've just made lists each file in it, so you can look at, stage or discard them one at a time, or the whole folder at once. A folder of more than 1,000 new files, like build output that should be ignored, stays one line.\n- **Starting Tributary while it's open** brings its window to the front, instead of opening a second one.\n- **On Windows, Settings → Apps names Tributary's publisher**, Bogdan Roatis, instead of \"github\".\n- **Why? on a failed check** sits on the same line as the check's name, in the pull request window and in a commit's details.",
